WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:29.339
OK, I want to get ahead of what is going to
be a barrage of absolute B.S. garbage math.

00:00:29.339 --> 00:00:31.710
This is what I'm calling a garbage math.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:37.060
You are going to be hit left and right by
garbage math over the next few months.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:41.510
And the goal of the garbage math will be for
you to think that Donald Trump deserves to

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:42.510
be reelected.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:46.990
And we've already been starting to see some
of what I'm calling garbage math with corona

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:47.990
virus for a while.

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:53.050
So, for example, when Trump lists talk about
the total number of tests that have been done

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:55.850
in the United States, that's garbage math.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.670
It doesn't tell us very much.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:01.860
Tests per day are what matter tests per capita
are, what matter.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.030
We're four months into this thing, right.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:07.619
The cumulative number of tests that have been
done tell us very, very little.

00:01:07.619 --> 00:01:13.979
Another example, over the last couple of months,
we've seen such economic destruction that

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:22.020
it now opens the door to wild, meaningless
claims about growth rates in certain industries,

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:24.369
which are already being touted as huge victories.

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:27.729
Now, I want to give you a really specific
example.

00:01:27.729 --> 00:01:30.150
There's been this headline floating around.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:33.450
Air travel surges, air travel and bookings
surge.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:34.810
One hundred and twenty percent.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:40.100
The New York Times actually wrote about this
when this started being touted.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.530
So understand when Corona virus hit.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:47.429
Air travel dropped by one estimate, 96 percent.

00:01:47.429 --> 00:01:54.390
So when you say air travel surges one hundred
and twenty percent, it has surged 120 percent

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.369
or roughly doubled from almost zero.

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:03.619
OK, so imagining that air travel is going
well completely ignores just basic math.

00:02:03.619 --> 00:02:05.060
Let's look at some numbers.

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:07.560
If you start with what would be one hundred
percent, right.

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:12.781
Like full air travel and you then experience
and 96 percent decrease to air travel, you

00:02:12.781 --> 00:02:17.530
end up with four percent of the air travel
you started with.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:18.650
You start with something.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.350
You take away 96 percent.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.930
You're left with four percent.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:29.140
If you then increase air travel by one hundred
and twenty percent, air travel is still down

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.300
91, one percent from where it was.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:36.910
This is just a common function of how percentages
and growth rates work.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:41.940
If you lose 50 percent of something, you need
to gain 100 percent to get back to where you

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:42.940
were.

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:47.340
If I have one hundred dollars in stock and
that stock loses 50 percent of its value.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.780
My hundred dollars is now worth fifty dollars
to get back to one hundred dollars.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:55.040
I need that 50 bucks to increase.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.520
One hundred percent.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:02.640
And this is the crux of a ton of the disinformation,
propaganda, propaganda, garbage, math that

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.850
you are going to be hit with over the next
few months as the recovery hopefully starts

00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:11.900
to happen, depending on how deadly coronavirus
remains and how bad Wave two is.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.370
You are going to see a lot of this stuff.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.660
I'll give you some other examples.

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:22.680
Jobs have so imploded that they will start
using job growth numbers that will be meaningless

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.990
because of the base numbers that are being
used.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:31.270
Understand that we lost more than 20 percent
of our workforce so far.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:38.770
We might recover five percent of the current
workforce and they will say, hey, we're already

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.740
a quarter of the way back.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.800
That's not how growth rates work.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:48.720
If you lose 20 percent of the workforce and
then you gain back five percent of that reduced

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.890
number, you're only gaining 19 percent of
what you lost.

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:54.890
Not 25.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.940
So understand how these growth rates work.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:04:03.110
The one that will really be weaponized is
probably going to be GDP.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:10.710
We in all likelihood are going to see historic
GDP loss numbers for the second quarter in

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:11.750
the United States.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:14.810
We're not going to get those numbers until
sometime in August.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:15.850
Most likely.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:21.389
So normally the numbers would be, for example,
like, oh, GDP in Q2 went up.

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:22.830
Point five percent.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:24.870
Usually you annualize that.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:31.650
So point five percent over four quarters would
be like ontrack for two percent GDP growth

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:32.650
for the year.

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:33.650
OK.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:37.020
The decline in the second quarter is going
to be massive.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:38.720
It could be 10 percent.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:39.720
Could be 12 percent.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.340
Could be 25 percent.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.060
Imagine it's 15 percent.

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:48.970
If you annualize that 15 percent, one quarter
loss, it would be like 60 percent for the

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:49.970
year.

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:54.189
It's not going to really tell us much because
in all likelihood, in the third quarter, if

00:04:54.189 --> 00:04:59.080
things go as as are sort of expected, we're
going to start to see a recovery.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:06.520
And remember that recovery in GDP would only
be a fraction of what was lost in the second

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:07.520
quarter.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:12.930
The headlines and Trump and others are going
to say, for example, imagine that we lose

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:20.009
15 percent in Q2 and gain five percent in
Q3 relative to those previous numbers.

00:05:20.009 --> 00:05:26.729
The trumpet will say, oh, my God, five percent
in a quarter, we're on track for 20 percent

00:05:26.729 --> 00:05:27.979
GDP growth.

00:05:27.979 --> 00:05:32.090
If you annualize it, even China doesn't grow
20 percent in a year.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:37.719
And of course, that would be wrong because
we will have just suffered one of the if not

00:05:37.719 --> 00:05:41.759
the greatest GDP loss in American American
history.

00:05:41.759 --> 00:05:46.689
And there would be no reason to annualize
third quarter GDP growth.

00:05:46.689 --> 00:05:51.349
If we have it, given that it is a year with
massive ups and downs.

00:05:51.349 --> 00:05:57.120
So the concern I have is that the timing of
that could be very, very pernicious.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:02.759
We are likely to learn the Q3 GDP numbers
at the end of October.

00:06:02.759 --> 00:06:05.969
That is days before the presidential election.

00:06:05.969 --> 00:06:11.490
And it is going to present the perfect opportunity
for Donald Trump to do what will essentially

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:20.240
be like 96 hours of propaganda, confusing
people about Q3 GDP numbers to justify his

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:21.240
reelection.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:22.240
And it may work.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:23.819
I'm trying to stay ahead of it.

00:06:23.819 --> 00:06:26.159
I don't want you to fall for the garbage math.

00:06:26.159 --> 00:06:32.139
And the reason why it's important to understand
is that this is one where there's a couple

00:06:32.139 --> 00:06:33.139
of different things.

00:06:33.139 --> 00:06:35.360
First of all, you don't want to get tricked
by bad math.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:38.949
We all want to have basic math and statistics
understanding.

00:06:38.949 --> 00:06:46.530
But number two, policymakers are going to
be using bad data to justify the policies

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:52.340
that they would be advocating for anyway and
pretending like it's where the facts are taking

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:53.340
us.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:57.150
When the reality is they'd be advocating for
the same policies no matter what, and they'd

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.990
be advocating for the reelection of Donald
Trump no matter what.

00:06:59.990 --> 00:07:00.990
They will claim victory.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:03.430
They will claim mission accomplished.

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:04.860
Understand growth rates.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:05.990
Understand percentages.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:08.370
Don't fall for the garbage math.

